#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cos_integral

#% text
# `cos_integral(x)` gives the cosine integral `Ci(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `Ci(x)` is `euler_gamma + log(x)` plus the integral of `(cos(t) - 1)/t` from 0 to `x` (DLMF 6.2.13); equivalently minus the integral of `cos(t)/t` from `x` to `inf`. It has a logarithmic singularity at 0.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values; a negative real argument gives a complex value.

#% item
# `cos_integral` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
cos_integral(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.33740392290096816

#% cell
cos_integral(10.0)
#% output
#= -0.04545643300445537

#% cell
n(cos_integral(1), 30)
#% output
#= 0.3374039229009681346626462038891508

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A negative argument:

#% cell
cos_integral(-1.0)
#% output
#= 0.33740392290096816 + 3.141592653589793im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(cos_integral(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x)/x

#% text
# The first zero:

#% cell
find_root(cos_integral(x) == 0, [x, 0.6])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.6165054856207163]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sin_integral`, `cosh_integral`, `exp_integral_ei`, `euler_gamma`
